22:27:55 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That’s a very fair follow-up, and I’m glad you asked, because it’s easy to interpret that choking sensation as water building up in your lungs. But what you're experiencing is almost certainly **not** liquid water pooling in your lower airways—it’s an irritation reflex from the upper respiratory tract. Let me unpack why that happens and why it can feel so alarming. ### The choking/coughing sensation is real—but it’s not drowning What you sense as “choking up” is usually your **throat and upper trachea** reacting to the hot, moist air, not water condensing deep in your lungs. Here are the likely culprits: 1. **Heat-triggered cough reflex:** Your larynx and trachea have heat-sensitive nerve endings (TRPV1 and others). Even in a comfortable sauna, inhaling air that’s significantly hotter than body temperature can directly stimulate these nerves, triggering a protective cough or a sensation of “tightness”/choking. It’s the same reason some people cough when stepping into a very hot shower. 2. **Humidity-induced mucus changes:** The sudden high humidity can cause the mucus in your nose and throat to thin out and start running. That post-nasal drip can tickle your larynx, making you feel like you’re choking on your own secretions—even if there’s no extra water from steam. 3. **Irritant-triggered bronchoconstriction:** In some people, hot humid air acts as an airway irritant, causing a temporary narrowing of the bronchial tubes (similar to what happens in exercise-induced asthma). This can give a sensation of chest tightness, difficulty breathing, and a dry cough that feels like choking. If you have mild undiagnosed asthma or reactive airways, a sauna can unmask it. 4. **Chemical irritants in the “steam”:** In a shower, what looks like pure steam is often mixed with tiny droplets of tap water that contain chlorine or chloramine disinfectants. When you inhale those aerosolized chemicals, they can be quite irritating to the mucous membranes of the throat and lungs, leading to coughing and a choking feeling. This is why some people cough more in a shower than in a clean sauna with just water on rocks. ### Why it still isn’t dangerous water buildup Let’s go back to the numbers: even breathing deeply in extreme conditions (80°C, saturated), the **maximum liquid water that could condense inside your lungs over an hour is only a few milliliters**—less than a teaspoon. That’s spread over a huge surface area (about 50–100 m² of lung tissue) and is continuously absorbed/cleared by the normal fluid layer and lymphatics. You would never feel it as a gurgling pool of water; the “wet” sensation is from your upper airway’s own mucus, not from condensed steam. ### What can you do about it? If this consistently happens to you, it’s not that steam is dangerously pooling water—it’s that your airways are particularly sensitive. You can try: - Breathe more slowly and, if possible, through your nose (which conditions the air better). - Lower the temperature slightly or move further from the steam source. - If it’s a shower, run the fan and consider using a filter that removes chlorine (if the problem is chemical irritation). - If you also feel chest tightness or wheezing outside of steam, or the cough is severe, it might be worth mentioning to a doctor to rule out mild asthma/reactive airway disease. So no, it’s not water building up in your lungs and you’re not in danger of drowning—but the coughing and choking feeling are genuine and likely come from irritation of your upper airways. You’re not alone; many people experience this, especially in highly chlorinated shower steam.
